<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      docs/plan/03 § Providers — the implementation assembly: "grains, reconcilers, the chart
      bindings". Two reconcilers, an action handler, a chart binding — and, since docs/plan/13
      § Artifact feeds landed, ONE GRAIN.

      ⚠ THIS HEADER USED TO OPEN "no grain (see the .Contracts .csproj)" AND "ITS ENTIRE REFERENCE
      SET IS ITS OWN .Contracts", AND BOTH SENTENCES STOPPED BEING TRUE ON THE SAME DAY. FeedGrain is
      the catalogue of CyberCloud.ContainerRegistry/feeds — what tenants put INTO a feed after it
      exists — and durable-grains.txt's Providers section says why the resource manager could not
      hold that for the provider. A grain needs Orleans' server assembly to derive from Grain and
      bind IPersistentState, and a reconciler that reaches a grain needs ForTenant, so the two
      packages below are here. The Sample provider's .csproj said "if a future provider needs Orleans
      in its implementation assembly, that is worth a second look before the package goes in"; the
      second look is this paragraph, and the answer is that a feed's data plane IS a grain plus a
      storage prefix, so reaching one is the whole of what its reconciler does.

      ⚠ WHAT DID NOT CHANGE. docs/plan/07 § The enforcement seam: "Providers never call the engine."
      That is still mechanical rather than reviewed — CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Contracts does not
      reference CyberCloud.Authorization.Contracts, so ICheckGrain is not nameable from here, and
      neither package below brings it in. CC1006 now polices this assembly for real: every GetGrain
      goes through ForTenant, and the analyzer reference below is what makes that a compile error
      rather than a review comment.

      ⚠ NO KubernetesClient. docs/plan/03 § Assembly graph rules, rule 3: nothing above
      CyberCloud.Kubernetes names a k8s.Models type. A harbor-operator `HarborCluster` has no
      k8s.Models type anyway — it is a custom resource — and building it as JsonObject rather than as
      a generated CRD model is what keeps that true without anyone having to remember it. It matters
      here for the same reason it did on mariadb-operator: goharbor.io is served at v1beta1, and a
      generated model would pin a beta shape into a compiled assembly.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <!-- Grain, IPersistentState, and the source generator over FeedState. -->
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.Server"/>
        <!-- IGrainFactory.ForTenant and GetTenantId — ADR-002; every grain reference here goes through them. -->
        <PackageReference Include="Orleans.Multitenant"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Providers.ContainerRegistry.Contracts/CyberCloud.Providers.ContainerRegistry.Contracts.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="CyberCloud.Analyzers — see CyberCloud.Core.csproj for why this is not a reference">
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../CyberCloud.Analyzers/CyberCloud.Analyzers.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
